bodyText:
Maine has no statute setting when rent is due, a grace period, or what happens when the due date falls on a weekend, so the lease controls those points.

A lease may say rent is paid without deduction or setoff, but Maine law lets a tenant subtract certain amounts from rent, and a lease cannot take those rights away. The main ones:
- if you fail to fix a condition that endangers health or safety and the reasonable cost is less than $500 or half a month's rent (whichever is greater), the tenant may notify you in writing, wait 14 days after a certified-mail notice (or less in an emergency), have it repaired professionally and deduct the cost after giving you an itemized statement (this repair-and-deduct right does not apply in an owner-occupied building of five or fewer units, or to damage the tenant caused);
- if a utility account in your name goes unpaid and the tenant pays the utility, the tenant may deduct that payment from rent;
- and if a tenant is taxed on the rented real estate, the tenant may keep half of the tax from rent unless you agreed otherwise.

A court can also reduce rent for habitability violations. Requiring a tenant to waive these rights is an unfair trade practice.

Many Maine notices must be in writing, and a lease cannot downgrade them to oral notice, because waivers of tenant rights are void.

In writing:
- every termination notice from you, and a tenant's notice ending a tenancy at will or ending a lease for your breach or as a victim;
- notice of a rent or mandatory recurring fee increase;
- notice at signing that a late fee may be charged;
- notice before an optional recurring fee starts;
- the smoking policy, radon notice and total price disclosure;
- the itemized statement of deposit deductions;
- the unclaimed-property notice;
- your notice that you are using a vacated unit while re-renting;
- and a waiver of the 30-day termination notice, which is valid only if written when the notice is given.

Tenants must give written notice of defects to reduce rent in your rent action, and written notice (or actual notice to whoever customarily collects your rent) to sue over habitability, and certified-mail written notice before repair-and-deduct; the nonpayment-eviction defense needs only your actual or constructive notice.

A tenant may report bedbugs orally or in writing, and entry notice need only be reasonable, so put both in writing anyway to create a record.

Maine has no single state form that must be the first page of a lease and no notice telling tenants whether a just-cause or rent-increase law covers the unit. Its required disclosures are separate items, most due before the tenant signs or pays a deposit:
- the radon notice on the state health department's form with the latest test date and result;
- the residential energy efficiency disclosure statement, signed by both, if the tenant will pay for an energy supply;
- your written smoking policy, in the lease or a separate notice, with the tenant's written acknowledgment;
- a signed total price disclosure listing rent, recurring fees, utility service costs and other costs, whenever the tenant pays a mandatory or optional recurring fee (without it the lease is unenforceable);
- whether an adjacent unit is infested with or being treated for bed bugs;
- the most recent private well test results within 10 days of results for a new tenant;
- written notice of the up-to-4% late fee if you will charge one;
- and, for pre-1978 housing, the federal lead disclosure and pamphlet.

The library's Maine disclosure acknowledgment clause collects these.

Since December 2025 the Attorney General must post, and update every odd-numbered year, a model residential lease that includes required disclosures and attachments; compare your lease with it.
